.TH DATE 1
.SH NAME
date \- print and set the date
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B date
.RB [ \-u ]
.RB "[ yymmddhhmm [ " . "ss ] ]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
If no argument is given, the current date and time are printed.
If an argument is given, the current date is set.
.I yy
is the last two digits of the year;
the first
.I mm
is the month number;
.I dd
is the day number in the month;
.I hh
is the hour number (24 hour system);
the second
.I mm
is the minute number;
.BI . ss
is optional and is the seconds.
For example:
.IP
date 10080045
.PP
sets the date to Oct 8, 12:45 AM.
The year, month and day may be omitted, the current
values being the defaults.
The system operates in GMT.
.I Date
takes care of the conversion to and from
local standard and daylight time.
.PP
.B \-u
works in GMT rather than in local time, both when setting and when printing.
.B Note:
v7's page documented neither the flag nor the fact that the program has always
had it.
.SH FILES
/usr/adm/wtmp to record time-setting.
.B Note:
there is no /usr/adm on this system, so the records are not written; see the
BESM-6 notes below.
.SH SEE ALSO
utmp(5), stime(2), ctime(3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
`No permission' if
you aren't the super-user and you try to change the date;
`bad conversion' if the date set is syntactically incorrect.
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.SS The two-digit year names 1970 through 2069
.B This is a deliberate change, and the only one here a user can see.
v7 added 1900 to the two digits and stopped, so no year after 1999 could be named
at all \- and
.B date 0001010000
did not fail: it meant 1 January 1900, seventy years before the epoch, out of
which the day count came back as a wrong date rather than as an error.  Here
.B 70
through
.B 99
mean 1970 through 1999, and
.B 00
through
.B 69
mean 2000 through 2069.  The pivot is 70 because that is where a
.I time_t
begins on this machine, so every year the window can name is one the clock can
hold.
.SS The machine is always GMT
.I TIMEZONE
and
.I DSTFLAG
in <sys/param.h> are both 0, the kernel's
.IR ftime (2)
hands those out unchanged, and there is no zoneinfo to read.  So
.B date
and
.B "date \-u"
print the same instant and differ only in where the zone name comes from \- which
means the DESCRIPTION's "conversion to and from local standard and daylight time"
is arithmetic that runs and adds nothing.  It is kept rather than removed, against
the day those two constants are not zero.
.SS A time_t is one word, so stime(2) takes a value
41 bits of seconds \- no 2038 problem \- and the gate shed an argument because of
it: v7's
.I time_t
was two PDP-11 words and its
.IR stime (2)
took a pointer to both.  Here it takes the number.  See
.IR stime (2),
and
.IR intro (2)
for why an argument count is a contract between three places.
.SS /usr/adm/wtmp does not exist on this image
v7 appends a pair of
.I utmp
records around a clock change, so that
.IR ac (1)
can account for the discontinuity.  The code is kept and its write is guarded, as
it always was, so nothing happens: root.manifest declares no /usr/adm, and the
directory is deliberately not created \- a file that grew on every clock change
would make the image-diffing tests non-reproducible.  It comes back with
.IR login (1)
and the rest of
.IR utmp (5).
